<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Jersika</h1><p class="st-meta">1190 CE – 1239 CE (approx.) · capital Jersika</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Orthodox principality of the Latgalians on the Daugava, ruled by King Visvaldis until the crusaders of Riga absorbed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Jersika was a hill fort on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Latvia</a> and the centre of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalian</a> principality linked to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-polotsk">Principality of Polotsk</a>. By about 1200 it was ruled by a prince the German sources call <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visvaldis">Vissewalde</a>, known in Latvian as Visvaldis, who was probably of mixed Latgalian and Rus' descent.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Jersika controlled trade on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> and ruled over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalians</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/selonians">Selonians</a>, some of whom were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christians with churches. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-livonia">Henry of Livonia</a> calls <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visvaldis">Visvaldis</a> a king. He allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> against the German crusaders who had founded Riga.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1209 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Bishop Albert of Riga</a> stormed and burned Jersika, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visvaldis">Visvaldis's</a> wife and forced him to become a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, receiving part of his land back as a fief. The rest of his territory passed gradually to Riga and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a>, and the principality had disappeared by about 1239.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-jersika.jpg" alt="Principality of Jersika" width="320" height="164"><figcaption>Image: Garais at Latvian Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jersika.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1190 CE – 1239 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jersika</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Visvaldis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latgalian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gerzika, Jersika, Kingdom of Jersika</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1214&amp;focus=principality-of-jersika" aria-label="Principality of Jersika on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5248 264 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Jersika lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5568 520L5630 540M5568 520L5520 501"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5630 540h0M5520 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5568 520h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5568 520h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jersika. <a href="/atlas?year=1214&amp;focus=principality-of-jersika">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

